Value equality:


- Signals are used to hold state, and you can react to their change. ❓But how do they decide that
    they have changed? Calling set or update on a signal doesn’t necessarily make it change: 🔥🔥if
    you set a value that is equal to the value it already has, then the signal will consider that
    it hasn’t changed.


- 🔥🔥This equality of two values, by default, is decided by calling Object.is. For objects, if
    the new value is === to the current value, no change happens. 🔥🔥But you can define a custom
    equality in case referential equality is not what you want.

    for example:
    1️⃣

        const count = signal(10);
    
    when you do:

        count.set(10); 
    
    🧠Angular compares the old value (10) with the new value (10) using Object.is() by default.

        Object.is(10, 10); // true
    
    🧠🧠Since they're considered equal, Angular says: "Nothing changed. I don't need to notify
    anything depending on this signal."

    -------------------------------------------------------

    2️⃣
        const user = signal({
            name: 'John'
        });

    Now, when you do:

        user.set({
            name: 'John'
        });

    Although the objects look identical, they are actually two different objects in memory.

        const user1 = { name: 'John' };
        const user2 = { name: 'John' };

        console.log(user1 === user2); // false
    
    🧠🧠So Angular considers the value changed.

    ⚠️⚠️Note that set() used here is not a JS setter. It's signal's .set().
    🧠🧠Signal is holding the reference to object { name: 'John' }. and when we replace the signal's
    value with .set(), signal changes from holding a reference to new value/object. That's why
    with set() we get two different objects.

    ------------------------------------------------------

    3️⃣ custom equality:
    🔥BUT if we don't want referential equality.
    Referential equality means asking:"Are these two variables pointing to the exact same object
    in memory?"

        const status = signal(
            {
                dirty: false,
                touched: false
            },
            {
            equal: (previousStatus, newStatus) =>
                previousStatus.dirty === newStatus.dirty && previousStatus.touched ===
                newStatus.touched
            }
        );

        // 🔥errorDisplayed will not re-evaluated if both values stay the same
        const errorDisplayed = computed(() => status().dirty || status().touched);
    
    OR

        const user = signal(
            { name: 'John', age: 20 },
            {
                equal: (a, b) => a.name === b.name && a.age === b.age
            }
        );

        user.set({ name: 'John', age: 20 });

    🧠🧠The objects have different references, but your custom equality function returns: true
    So Angular considers them equal and doesn't trigger updates.